💡 **SUGGESTION: GitLab `prStatus` is not memoized per tick — the GitHub twin memoizes, so a GitLab tick re-fetches MR status it already read**

The GitHub provider memoizes status reads per (owner, repo, PR) via `_ghPrStatusMemo` (`js/common/smProvider.js:1096-1098`); the GitLab twin calls `_glPrStatus` bare:

```js
prStatus: function (mrNumber) {
    return _glPrStatus(owner, repo, mrNumber);
},
```

Each call costs two bridge round-trips (`gitlab_get_mr` + `gitlab_get_mr_pipelines`). On a GitLab tick the readers multiply with no shared memo:

- every rule whose query filters on checks/merge state re-enriches the whole open-MR list (`_enrichPrItemsWithStatus` → one `prStatus` per MR per rule — `rework-on-red-ci` and `review-external-once` both do this today);
- the enrich workers run without a snapshot preseed (the documented GitLab gap), so each worker builds a fresh provider and re-fetches statuses another rule already read;
- `benchDeadHeadParks` / `validationRollupGreen` probes add per-candidate reads on top.

`_glListOpenPrs` and `_glOpenIssues` already solve this exact problem with `ioCachePut(owner, repo, …)` — the same one-liner cache for MR status (keyed `owner/repo:mrStatus:<iid>`, dropped by the existing `ioCacheDrop` invalidation points) would cap the tick at one fetch per MR.

Volume is bounded today (8 dispatch-only rules, small `limit`s), so this is polish, not a blocker — but it's the same ioCache pattern this PR already established twice, applied to the one provider read that still bypasses it.

